= B3 Outline

Introduce the incentives of buyers and sellers in markets. Only indirectly touch individual PS and CS welfare as a way to measure incentives.

== Sep 22 | Proposed, Seven Scenes

+ The basic exchange. A price between MC and MB can facilitate the trade. What price should they choose?
+ The bidding war. Switching drives the price around. Stable means no one wants to switch.
+ Multiple trades. Prices must be equal for prices to be stable.
+ Buyers. At one price, this is how many would buy. The rest offer their MB.
+ Sellers. The same in a separate plaza. Their asks are their MC.
+ Finding equilibrium. Shortage at \$3, excess at \$6, no one left out at \$4.
+ The graph and the algebra.

Built as the opening of the B4 animation. Used Sep 23 as the review in B4.

== Sep 21 | Used in Class

+ Recap demand and supply. The price is left floating.
+ The first exchange, Gary and Molly. Expenditure, CS, revenue, PS.
+ Amanda-Grace bids the price up.
+ The market grows one person at a time and settles.
+ Name equilibrium. Then the graph, the algebra, and Exercise Q1.
+ Test \$3 and \$6. Shortage, excess, stable. Exercise Q2.
+ PPF tieback.

We only got through to the first exercise question.

== Original Script

+ Recap demand and supply. The price is left floating.
+ Shortage at a low price. A buyer jumps the line.
+ Excess at a high price. A seller undercuts.
+ The price that doesn’t move. Qd equals Qs.
+ The algebra. Then one graph and the star.
+ Stable.
+ PPF tieback.

== Nuances

- The notes are prep material before class, not a script.
- There was too much. At least one part of an exercise should come up front.
- Core scenes instead of one long animation.
- We don’t need the growing of the market. Teach bidding, switching, the single price, and deliberation on small stages, then show them for one or two players in the large one.
- Stay head on for multiple trades. One buyer highlighted, two options in two boxes.
- One person per bar. If someone wants more than one, they show up twice.
- The price window only appears in the first exchange.
- Supply and demand separate to start. Stack them vertically to show the quantities are equal.
- Spend plenty of time on equilibrium on the graph.
- Excess, not surplus.
- It should set up taxes, international trade, and externalities.
- We spent too long on the specifics of the market dynamics.

~

= B4 Outline

Do welfare at the market level.~

+ Show one exchange, setting the price very low with a price control, thinking through quantity exchanged, and so on.
+ Then thinking about incentives in the price control, and then a move to equilibrium, and how welfare changed.
+ Then more quickly show a high price, doing it with TS maybe here.
+ Then force a few more people to exchange and see what happens to TS.
+ Then do first welfare theorem.

~

== Sep 23 | Used in Class

+ Review B3 in seven stages, with Exercise B3 Q2 before equilibrium.
+ The gains from trade. CS and PS on one exchange become total surplus.
+ A price floor at \$6. The lost gains, then one pair that can’t trade.
+ A price ceiling at \$3. The lost gains, then one pair that can’t trade. Exercise B4 Q1.
+ Give that pair the trade. The market without controls allows all beneficial trades.
+ Trades beyond equilibrium. Negative TS.
+ First welfare theorem. Exercise B4 Q2.

== Sep 22 | Proposed, Back Half

+ How much does the market benefit buyers and sellers. Total surplus.
+ Can we increase it by raising the price? DWL is the TS we could have had. Zoom into a pair that doesn’t trade because of the price control.
+ Then lower the price. Show the DWL again with a specific pair.
+ Give them the trade. TS is maximized by allowing all these people to trade.
+ We don’t want anything more. Force the next pair to trade and they lose benefit. Negative TS.

== Sep 22 | Notes

+ Is the market even good?
+ A price ceiling at \$3. Buyers can’t jump the line. How much is sold?
+ Review CS, PS, and total surplus.
+ Welfare at \$3. DWL.
+ A price floor at \$6.
+ The social planner sets the price.
+ First welfare theorem and its two caveats. Efficiency.

== Lecture 08

+ Is the market even good?
+ The government decides the price should be low. The shortage persists.
+ How much is sold? What’s the welfare?
+ DWL.
+ The government mandates a high price.
+ First welfare theorem, two caveats, Adam Smith.
+ Surplus value intuition.

== Original Script

+ Consumer surplus.
+ Producer surplus.
+ Efficiency and the social planner.
+ Price controls.

== Nuances

- Price controls motivate the question of what’s best.
- Start at equilibrium, since B3 just showed that setting.
- The back half should carry the decisions and styling of the front half.
- Comparing welfare changes under a price control to equilibrium may live better in B5.
- The interpretation is not that price controls are bad. Welfare analysis is a toolset for evaluating them.
- Surplus means value created. That’s why we say excess.
- The theorem needs competitive markets and no externalities. Part E and Part C.

